\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\begin{document}
\section{Introduction}
Traffic forecasting underpins modern mobility platforms.
Accurate predictions let dispatchers rebalance fleets before congestion forms.
Graph neural networks model road topology naturally.
Yet most benchmarks ignore sensor dropout.
We argue robustness is the \textbf{primary bottleneck} for deployment.
This paper studies forecasting under missing data.
\section{Related Work}
Early work used Kalman filters and ARIMA variants.
Recent work adopts spatio-temporal graph convolutions.
\subsection{Deep Models}
Attention-based forecasters dominate recent leaderboards.
Diffusion-based forecasters report sharpness gains.
\subsection{Robustness}
Dropout during training improves resilience mildly.
\section{Preliminaries}
A road network is a directed graph $\mathcal{G} = (\mathcal{V}, \mathcal{E})$.
Each node carries a multivariate series $\mathbf{x}_v^{(t)}$.
The task predicts the next $H$ steps for all nodes.
\section{Method}
Our model has three components:
\begin{itemize}
\item A masked encoder with learnable sensor embeddings.
\item A temporal attention block with causal masking.
\item A reconstruction head trained with huber loss.
\end{itemize}
The \textbf{masking schedule} is the key design choice.
We sample masks per batch, not per epoch.
\section{Experiments}
We evaluate on two public datasets.
\begin{itemize}
\item METR-LA: 207 sensors over four months.
\item PEMS-BAY: 325 sensors over six months.
\end{itemize}
Metrics are MAE, RMSE, and MAPE at 15/30/60 minutes.
Baselines include DCRNN, STGCN, and Graph WaveNet.
Our model leads under 50\% sensor dropout.
The margin is \textbf{most pronounced} at long horizons.
\section{Conclusion}
Mask-aware training materially improves robustness.
Future work extends the study to streaming settings.
\end{document}

